Sources:
- P. C. Caldwell, M. A. Merrifield, P. R. Thompson, Sea level measured by tide gauges from global
  oceans — the Joint Archive for Sea Level holdings (NCEI Accession 0019568), Version 5.5, NOAA
  National Centers for Environmental Information (2015), doi:10.7289/V5V40S7W
  <https://uhslc.soest.hawaii.edu/datainfo/>
Evidence class (rule 10): check
Where the Earth's radius enters:
- frequencies of the harmonic lines: not the radius: the harmonic fit takes the frequency of each
  tidal line from the mean motions of the Moon and the Sun (Meeus); the periods read off the
  spectrum and the spring-neap cycle use the clock alone
What would vindicate the flat model (rule 5): A tide gauge whose record has one high water per lunar
  day and no semidiurnal line at 12 h 25 min anywhere, or a fortnightly spring-neap cycle that does
  not follow the phases of the Moon, would favour a local Moon pulling water under itself over a
  plane.
